If $d \ge L$, then
$\|Q_n - Q_\ell\| = d\cdot \frac{\|Q_n-Q_{n-d}\|}{d}
 \le (n-\ell)\max_{L\le m\le n}\frac{\|Q_n-Q_{n-m}\|}{m}$.

From Definition~\ref{def:U},
$\frac{U_{n,k}}{n} = \frac{\alpha}{n}\sum_{m=0}^{n-1}\hat{\rho}_{m,k}
 - \hat{\rho}_{n,k} + \frac{M_{n,k}}{n}$.

By Lemma~\ref{lem:M_lln} the last term is $o(1)$; by Lemma~\ref{lem:rho_converges}
$\hat{\rho}_{n,k}\to u_k$, and by Cesàro
$\frac1n\sum_{m<n}\hat{\rho}_{m,k}\to u_k$.

if arm $k$ is eventually fed \emph{only} by forced exploration, then its count is regular
    against the \emph{deterministic} schedule, $N_{n,k}/h(n)\to1$
    (\texttt{AlphaRAR.pullCount\_div\_sched\_tendsto\_one});

\emph{Upper}
(\texttt{pullCount\_le\_sched\_of\_fe\_only}): at a forced-exploration round the selected arm is
itself under-explored, so one pull leaves it at $h(m)+1\le h(n)+1$.

$S$ is a martingale (Lemma~\ref{lem:hw_martingale}) with bounded increments $|\Delta S_j|=|Y_j|\le c$;
its quadratic variation is $\langle S\rangle_n=\sum_{j<n}\E[Y_j^2]$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:hw_qv}), so
$\langle S\rangle_n\ge w\,n\to\infty$.
